GARLIC is a program for calling runs of homozygosity in genotype data.  It implements the ROH calling method of Pemberton et al. AJHG (2012) and Blant et al. (2017).

Citations:

A Blant, et al. (2017) BMC genomics 18 (1), 928.
ZA Szpiech, et al. (2017) Bioinformatics 33 (13), 2059-2062.
TJ Pemberton, et al. (2012) AJHG, 91: 275–292

**NOTE TO WINDOWS USERS: garlic no longer links FIGTree or ANN, so ann_figtree_version.dll and figtree.dll are no longer needed. The KDE evaluates its Gaussian sum directly; see the CHANGES entry below.

This ROH calling pipeline has four phases.

I.		Window-based LOD score calculation
II.		Kernel Density Estimation of LOD score distribution
III.		Assembly of ROH
IV.		Gaussian Mixture Modeling of ROH size distribution

Input files:

Genotypes come from EITHER a VCF (--vcf) or a tped/tfam pair (--tped/--tfam).
The two are mutually exclusive.

vcf (--vcf; plain, gzip, or BGZF as written by bgzip/bcftools):
	Biallelic SNVs with a GT field are used; multiallelic sites, indels,
	symbolic ALTs (*, <NON_REF>) and sites with no GT in FORMAT are skipped
	and counted in the log.  The ALT allele is the one counted, so allele
	frequencies may come out as 1-f relative to the same data in a tped --
	this changes no ROH call, and a .freq.gz file is interchangeable between
	the two readers because readFreqData re-orients on the ALLELE column.

	A VCF names its own samples in its #CHROM line, so --tfam is not
	accepted with --vcf; use --pop for population labels.  Chromosomes must
	be grouped (all records for a chromosome contiguous), which is what
	`bcftools sort` produces.  Genotypes must be diploid, and under --phased
	every call must use '|'.

	Genetic positions are not in a VCF, so --weighted and --cm still need
	--map.

tped/tfam (required unless --vcf is given): 
	http://zzz.bwh.harvard.edu/plink/data.shtml#tr

pop (optional; with --vcf it is the only way to set population labels):
	<sample id> <population> [sex]

	Whitespace-separated, '#' comments and blank lines skipped, .gz allowed.
	Rows are matched BY SAMPLE ID, never by position.  Note the column order
	is <id> <pop>, the opposite of a tfam's first two columns; a tfam-ordered
	file is diagnosed as such.  The optional third column is PLINK sex coding
	(1 male, 2 female, 0 or -9 unknown) and is used only to make the
	sex-chromosome warning specific.

map (required when --weighted or --cm are set):
	<chr> <snpid> <genetic pos> <physical pos>
	http://zzz.bwh.harvard.edu/plink/data.shtml#map 
	(negative positions will cause errors)

tgls (optional):
	<chr> <snpid> <unused> <physical pos> <GL ind1> <GL ind2> ... <GL indN>

	Per-genotype likelihoods should be given as either a phred-scaled (--gl-type PL) or a log10-scaled (--gl-type GL) representing the probability that the genotype is correct or a phred-scaled likelihood that the genotype is wrong (--gl-type GQ).  For example, if p = 0.999 is the probability that the genotype is correct, then PL = -10*log10(p) = 0.00434511774018, GL = log10(p) = -0.000434511774018, and GQ = -10*log10(1-p) = 30.

	*** THESE ARE NOT THE VCF PL AND GL FIELDS. ***  A VCF normalises PL and
	GL so the CALLED genotype's value is exactly 0, which under the
	definitions above means an error rate of 0.  Dropping VCF PLs or GLs into
	a tgls file is now rejected with an error rather than silently producing
	a LOD of -16 per heterozygote (or, for an all-integer PL file, calling
	nothing at all).  --gl-type GQ is the one form whose VCF values can be
	used here directly, because GQ needs no normalisation.

	For VCF-derived likelihoods use --vcf --gl-type PL/GL instead: that path
	reads the whole FORMAT array and computes the posterior, which is the
	only correct use of a normalised PL.  vcf2tped.pl is deprecated for this
	reason.

freq (optional, output during computation, can be generated without running whole pipeling with --freq-only): 
	CHR SNP POS ALLELE FREQ
	<chr> <locus ID> <allele> <freq>
	
centromere file (required if no --build is set):
	<chr> <centromere start> <centromere end>

GARLIC's only external dependency is zlib (http://zlib.net/), which comes from the system
on Linux and macOS -- the Makefile links -lz and there is nothing to obtain or vendor.
Threading is std::thread from the C++11 standard library rather than pthreads called
directly; on Linux libstdc++ implements it over pthreads, so the Makefile still passes
-pthread there, but no pthreads library is named or vendored on any platform.  A static
Linux build additionally wraps -lpthread in --whole-archive, because libstdc++ decides at
run time whether threading is available by testing a weak symbol that a static link can
otherwise leave out.  win32 builds take zlib from the MinGW toolchain as well
(mingw-w64-x86_64-zlib under MINGW64, mingw-w64-i686-zlib under MINGW32), and require a
MinGW GCC configured with the POSIX threading model, which is what supplies std::thread
there.

GNU GSL and FIGTree were previously required and are no longer used.  FIGTree was replaced
by a direct Gaussian sum, which made the KDE reproducible; GSL was replaced by
src/garlic-math.cpp, which provides the ten routines garlic called and reproduces each of
them bit for bit, so results are unchanged.  That removed 44 MB of per-platform static
archives and vendored headers from the repository, and with them the main obstacle to
building on a cluster.

---CHANGES---
2.1.0 - Low-coverage sequence data.

This release adds genotype-likelihood input for low-coverage sequence data (--gl-beagle), a
per-individual information report (<out>.ind.tsv), an automatic cutoff chosen within strata
of individuals of similar depth (--stratify-cutoff), and a mask of regions to drop
(--exclude-regions). It also changes how the automatic LOD cutoff finds the two modes of the
LOD score density, which moves the cutoff in cohorts where the previous rule took the wrong
peaks. Output formats are those of 2.0.0, with <out>.ind.tsv and three fields of .params.json
added.

*** BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: the automatic LOD cutoff identifies the two modes of the window LOD
density by their sign. *** The non-autozygous mode is the tallest peak below zero and the
autozygous mode is the tallest peak above zero. The cutoff is the minimum of the density
between them, as before. A peak counts only if it reaches 2% of the height of the tallest
peak and persists over half the KDE mode span. The previous rule took the two most persistent
peaks. However, its persistence count saturated, so ties were broken by height with no
constraint on sign, and a small bump in the tail of the non-autozygous component could be
taken as a mode. On the bundled chr21 example the cutoff moves from -55.7 to -0.33, and the
calls from 22 Mb to 3.6 Mb per individual. The genome-wide example is unchanged at 1.88. A
density with no peak above zero (unimodal-null) now completes with no calls and a warning
that names --lod-cutoff and --stratify-cutoff, and .params.json writes lod_cutoff as null. A
density with no peak below zero stops and asks for --lod-cutoff. .params.json records the
verdict and both modes (lod_density_verdict, lod_mode_nonautozygous, lod_mode_autozygous).
The substitution of 0 for a valley more than --winsize from zero is gone, since a valley is
now defined by the two modes it lies between. With --stratify-cutoff, an individual's
non-autozygous mode is its lowest peak below zero that reaches 10% of the height of its
tallest. manual/lod-mode-rule.tex gives the derivation and the comparison with the previous
rule.

--gl-type documents that none of its forms suit low-coverage sequence data (below roughly
4x). Each reduces to one symmetric error rate per genotype, and the dominant low-depth error,
allelic dropout, is directional: it turns heterozygotes into homozygotes, which is what
autozygosity looks like. In simulation at 0.5-1x a GQ-derived error called essentially the
whole genome autozygous. Hard calls with --error degrade more gracefully. However, a caller
that applies a population prior at one or two reads (bcftools call -m by default) makes
heterozygous calls the reads do not support, and those calls lose the automatic cutoff by
about 2x. With maximum-likelihood calls (bcftools call -m -G - -P 0) the automatic cutoff
held at 1x and 2x in the pedigree and bottleneck cohorts of the read-level benchmark. A
genotype-likelihood input (--gl-beagle, below) is the intended route.

--gl-beagle reads genotype likelihoods in beagle format (ANGSD -doGlf 2) and is the input for
low-coverage sequence data. The per-site term of the LOD score is averaged over the three
genotypes with the reads' likelihood as the weight, instead of being evaluated at a called
genotype: an uncertain site contributes little evidence rather than confident wrong evidence,
a site with no reads (a flat triplet) contributes exactly 0, and a triplet that names one
genotype gives exactly the called-genotype value, so a beagle file of called genotypes
reproduces a TPED run byte for byte (the suite checks this). --error keeps its meaning.
Allele frequencies are estimated by EM over the likelihoods, per population, which is
unbiased at low depth where frequencies from called genotypes are pulled toward the major
allele; --freq-file (a column of ANGSD's .mafs.gz will do) overrides them. --tfam is required
for IDs, labels and sex, matched by position. Give LIKELIHOODS, not -doPost/-doGeno
posteriors, which have the Hardy-Weinberg prior already folded in. In the resampling
simulation this holds the called autozygous fraction at its true value down to 2x. In the
read-level benchmark it held down to 1x on long planted runs, so how far down it holds
depends on run length and on how much autozygosity the cohort carries, not on depth alone.
Cohorts of mixed depth are over-called in their shallow members unless --stratify-cutoff is
used. See the manual, "Genotype likelihood input". A site whose EM frequency implies less
than half a minor-allele copy among the individuals with reads is treated as fixed and
dropped, as a called site with no minor allele is: the EM stops short of 0 on a monomorphic
site, and such sites, kept with a frequency of 1e-7, gave every window they filled a LOD of
about 0, which is above the usual (negative) automatic cutoff -- an invariant region, or a
beagle file written without a site list, was called autozygous in every individual.

Every run writes <out>.ind.tsv, a per-individual information report. A window LOD is a sum
over its sites, and a site with no information for an individual (missing genotype, or a
flat likelihood triplet) contributes exactly 0, so two individuals with the same window size
can be summing very different amounts of evidence. Per individual: the fraction of
informative sites, the median and 5th percentile of informative sites per window, the
observed/expected heterozygote ratio over windows whose LOD is below 0 (a cutoff-free
stand-in for "not autozygous"), and, for --gl-beagle, dropout_hat, a profile-likelihood
estimate of P(true het observed as hom) that is 0 without allelic dropout and rises with it.
garlic warns when an individual's 5th percentile is below half the window size. The het
ratio is a sample-quality statistic for called data and NOT a low-depth estimator (it reads
~0.5 at 2x with no bias); dropout_hat is. Both carry sampling noise of about
1/sqrt(n_het_expected), which the table states. Nothing is corrected from this report.

--stratify-cutoff chooses the automatic LOD cutoff within strata of individuals that carry the
same evidence per window. The pooled cutoff assumes everyone does; in a cohort of mixed depth
the shallow individuals have both LOD modes compressed toward zero and one cutoff calls most of
their genome (1.6x the called-genotype length for the 2x third of a simulated 2x/4x/30x cohort).
The location of an individual's non-autozygous LOD mode measures that evidence precisely (sd
1.3 LOD within the bundled cohort, 18-29 LOD between depth classes), so individuals are sorted
by it, neighbours whose modes differ by less than a quarter of the smaller spread share a
stratum, and the pooled rule runs within each stratum. A uniform cohort is one stratum and gets
exactly the pooled cutoff (byte-identical calls; the suite checks it). Per-individual mode,
spread, stratum and applied cutoff go to <out>.ind.tsv; .params.json records lod_cutoff as the
stratum count and flags it. Strata of fewer than three individuals take the largest stratum's
cutoff, with a warning. Without the flag, garlic warns when the cohort would form more than one
stratum. What stratification removes is the pooling artefact; the noisier emission at low depth
remains (1.3x at 2x in that simulation) and no cutoff can undo it.

--exclude-regions <bed> drops every site inside the regions of a standard BED file before
anything is computed -- a mappability or repeat mask, segmental duplications, any region where
calls are not to be trusted. On every chromosome, autosomes included; chromosomes the file names
that are not in the data are ignored, a file covering no site warns. Dropped sites are gone from
the frequency file and every window; the FROH denominator is not reduced by them (unlike --par),
since a run can span a short masked region. This is the one correction garlic applies for
mapping bias, the regional part. The read-level part (ALT read fraction below one half at
heterozygotes) was simulated at 0.45-0.35 in half a cohort and did not move the calls at 2x and
above under the likelihood emission; two emission corrections reproduced the uncorrected result,
so neither ships, and dropout_hat in <out>.ind.tsv reports it instead. The manual's "Reference
and mapping bias" lists what belongs upstream (alt-aware mapping, -baq, read-length and quality
filters, damage rescaling, unbiased frequencies).

2.0.0 - Correctness, performance, and command line work.

A major version because this release is NOT backward compatible with files written by
v1.1.6a: .roh.bed now emits conforming BED coordinates (chromStart is 0-based, so it is
one less than before and chromEnd - chromStart is the length), the F_ROH table is one row
per individual instead of one per size class, and a run with more than one population
label writes one set of outputs per population instead of one pooled set.  Scripts that
parse those files need the changes described below.  Note that v1.1.6a was released more
than once with different .roh.bed coordinates, so the version in a file's track line does
not tell you which convention it uses; --roh-file works it out from the file itself.

*** BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a sex chromosome is no longer analysed as an autosome, and a run that
contains one stops until it is told what to do with it. ***  A hemizygous genotype is written
as a homozygous call, so analysing a sex chromosome as an autosome calls it as one run
spanning its whole length in every heterogametic individual and inflates FROH.  Earlier
versions warned and carried on, which is how such a number reaches a figure.

--sex-system takes xy, zw or none and supplies the one thing no amount of genotype data can:
which sex CODE is heterogametic.  PLINK codes 1 male and 2 female whatever the species' sex
determination system is, so in a ZW species the HOMOGAMETIC sex is coded 1.  Leaving the flag
unset is not the same as none -- unset means nothing has been said, and none asserts that
every chromosome is diploid in every individual.  The conventional names follow from the
system; --sex-chr, --sex-chr-degenerate and --haploid-chr name anything else.  A chromosome's
role does not depend on the system: X and Z are both the shared chromosome, Y and W both the
degenerate one.  Only the shared chromosome is analysed; the others, and the mitochondrion,
cannot carry a run of homozygosity in anybody and are dropped.

The numbers 23, 24, 25 and 26 are X, Y, the pseudoautosomal region and the mitochondrion under
PLINK's coding of HUMAN data, and ordinary autosomes in any species with that many chromosomes
-- chickens have 39 pairs.  garlic resolves them only when --build names a human assembly, and
otherwise refuses rather than picking a reading.  --autosomes-only now drops every
non-autosomal role rather than the four names X, Y, 23 and 24.

On the shared sex chromosome, allele frequencies are computed over everyone with the
heterogametic sex contributing one allele and the homogametic sex two, and runs of homozygosity
are called only in the individuals who are diploid there.  The LOD score cutoff, the window
size and the size-class boundaries are estimated on the autosomes and applied to it; garlic
reports what its own windows would have given, so the assumption is visible.  Adding a sex
chromosome to a run does not move a single autosomal call.

--sexchr-lod-cutoff and --sexchr-winsize override the two the diagnostic is about, for the
shared sex chromosome alone.  --sexchr-winsize requires --sexchr-lod-cutoff, since a cutoff
belongs to the window size it was estimated at.  Both are recorded in <out>.params.json as
sexchr_winsize and sexchr_lod_cutoff, holding what was applied whether or not the flags were
given.

--froh-denominator chooses what <out>.froh.tsv divides by.  analyzed, the default and what
garlic has always done, is the span the markers cover, less the centromere and anything --par
excluded; it moves with the marker set, so FROH from two arrays are not comparable.  assembly
is the full length of each analysed chromosome with nothing subtracted, which is what published
denominators are -- the hg19 autosomes sum to 2,881,033,286 bp and chrX is 155,270,560 bp, the
numbers Cotter et al. (2024) used.  On the bundled example the two differ by about 5%.

The sum is over the chromosomes that were ANALYSED, with the assembly supplying each one's
length; it is not a sum over the assembly.  --chr therefore divides by those chromosomes alone,
and a contig carrying no data is never looked up -- which is why a VCF full of decoys, unplaced
scaffolds and alt haplotypes needs no filtering.  A chromosome that was analysed with no length
available stops the run rather than falling back to its marker span.

Lengths come from --chr-lengths, from --build, or from a VCF's ##contig header, in that order.
--chr-lengths takes two columns, chromosome and length, and accepts a samtools faidx .fai
unchanged; it is the way to use this on a non-human assembly and on TPED input.  The built-in
tables are the GRC assembly reports' own numbers, recorded in centromeres/chr_lengths.txt.
assembly is refused with --cm.

Whenever lengths are available garlic checks that no marker lies past the end of its
chromosome, which is how a wrong --build announces itself -- an error under assembly, where
those lengths are the denominator, and a warning otherwise.  A VCF header and --build that
disagree on a length stop the run: one of them is not this assembly.

--sexchr-lod-cutoff and --sexchr-winsize are the escape hatch, for the shared sex chromosome
alone.  The autosomal estimates are the default rather than the only option, and the
diagnostic garlic already reports is where a replacement cutoff comes from.  --sexchr-winsize
requires --sexchr-lod-cutoff: a LOD score cutoff belongs to the window size it was estimated
at, and carrying the autosomal one onto windows of another size gives plausible-looking
calls that mean nothing.  Both are recorded in <out>.params.json as sexchr_winsize and
sexchr_lod_cutoff, holding what was applied whether or not the flags were given, and
--load-params replays them.

--weighted estimates its LD matrix on the shared sex chromosome from the individuals who are
diploid there, since a half call carries no genotype for either LD statistic to use.  With
--ld-subsample the subsample for that chromosome is drawn from those individuals, so the
number asked for is the number that informs it rather than the fraction of them that happens
to be diploid.  --tgls needs nothing said about it: a half call carries no genotype, so the
LOD score takes its default branch and never reads the error rate.  The same is true of GQ,
PL and GL read from a VCF, which matters because a haploid PL array holds one value per
allele -- two at a biallelic site, not the three a diploid site has.

A check that always runs establishes who is diploid there, from heterozygosity
(--het-rate-bounds) or from haploid calls where the file provides them.  It verifies a recorded
sex, infers an unrecorded one, and writes <out>.sexcheck.tsv.  Heterozygous calls in an
individual with one copy of the chromosome are impossible and are discarded and counted; if
more than half the individuals with a recorded sex contradict it, garlic stops, because that
means either --sex-system is inverted or the chromosome was called as diploid.

A VCF may write hemizygous genotypes as one allele.  On a declared sex chromosome that is now
read as a hemizygous call rather than rejected; elsewhere it is still an error.

FROH is reported for the autosomes and for the sex chromosome separately, each with its own
denominator, and is NA rather than zero for an individual that cannot be autozygous there.
The two are not summed.  The table also changes shape: one row per individual with the metrics
across columns, replacing one row per individual per size class.

--par and --par-file exclude pseudoautosomal regions, any number of them per chromosome, given
as coordinates rather than by a chromosome code.  For the human X, --build supplies them from
the Genome Reference Consortium's definition for that assembly, filed under whatever the data
calls the chromosome; see centromeres/par_regions.txt for the coordinates and where they came
from.  --par replaces them and --par none keeps them all.

Chromosome names are matched case-insensitively and ignoring a leading chr, so --chr x finds
chrX and a --build table matches a file that spells its chromosomes as bare numbers.  Two
chromosomes whose names differ only that way are an error, and so is the same name appearing
twice, which means one chromosome whose rows are not contiguous.

*** BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: input with more than one population label is now analysed one
population at a time, and the output file names change accordingly. ***  The LOD score is
parameterised by population allele frequency, so pooling distinct populations inflates
heterozygosity relative to any one of them and biases the ratio for all of them.  Earlier
versions pooled every individual and printed a warning telling the user to run each
population separately; garlic now does that itself.  Each population gets allele frequencies
from its own individuals, its own site filtering, its own automatically selected window size,
LOD cutoff and size-class boundaries, and its own random stream derived from --seed.  A value
given explicitly on the command line is not re-derived, so --winsize, --overlap-frac,
--lod-cutoff and --size-bounds apply to every population.  Analysing several populations
together gives each of them exactly what analysing it alone gives; they do not interact.

With ONE population nothing changes: the file names, the numbers and the log are what they
always were.  With more than one, the label is inserted before the extension --
<out>.<POP>.roh.bed, <out>.<POP>.froh.tsv, <out>.<POP>.ind.tsv, <out>.<POP>.params.json,
<out>.<POP>.<winsize>SNPs.kde -- while .log, .error and .freq.gz are written once for the run.
Anything that globs <out>.roh.bed will need to account for that.

--pool-populations restores the previous behaviour exactly: every individual analysed
together, pooled frequencies, unlabelled file names.  Its output is byte-identical to the
previous version's on the same input.

Note for users of PLINK-derived files: column 1 of a TFAM is garlic's population label, but
PLINK calls it the FAMILY ID, and it is often one family -- sometimes one sample -- per value.
Such a file will now produce one analysis per family.  garlic warns when the smallest
population has fewer than five individuals, and when there are more than two populations per
individual on average, but it cannot tell the two situations apart with certainty.  Use --pop
to supply real population labels, or --pool-populations to ignore them.

--freq-file now accepts one frequency column per population, matched by the column header, so
the column order need not match the order the populations appear in the input.  A file with a
single frequency column still applies to every population whatever that column is called, so
frequency files from earlier versions keep working.  garlic writes the same format: both the
analysis and --freq-only produce a .freq.gz that can be given straight back with --freq-file
to reproduce the run.

A population that cannot be analysed -- too few ROH for the size-class GMM, or a LOD cutoff
that cannot be selected -- is named in the error, and the run stops rather than writing a
partial set of results that looks complete.

*** NOT BACKWARD COMPATIBLE: .roh.bed coordinates have moved by one base. *** BED
chromStart is 0-based and chromEnd is exclusive, but garlic wrote the 1-based position of
the ROH's first variant, so chromEnd minus chromStart came out one less than the length in
column 5 and a genome browser drew every ROH one base short. chromStart is now 0-based, so
chromEnd minus chromStart equals column 5. Coordinates from earlier versions are shifted by
one relative to these. Reading such a file back is handled: --roh-file recovers the
convention from the file rather than from its version string, because v1.1.6a was released
with both.

--features counts classified genotypes inside and outside the calls, replacing
count_features_in_roh.pl and count_damaging_genotypes_in_roh.pl, which have been removed.
The classification is four columns -- <chr> <pos> <allele> <class> -- with an arbitrary
number of arbitrary class labels, and a site may carry more than one so that two annotation
schemes can classify the same variant.  The old format's first column packed chromosome and
position as chr:pos; convert a file with

  awk '{split($1,a,":"); print a[1], a[2], $3, $4}' old.txt > new.txt

and note that the classified allele is the third column, which is where those scripts read
it from as well.

Output is <out>.counts.tsv, one row per individual, with three numbers for every (class,
bucket): hom, homozygous for the classified allele; het, one copy; and n, the classified
sites the individual has a genotype at.  n is new and it is the point -- without a
denominator, "more deleterious homozygotes inside runs" cannot be separated from "more sites
inside runs".  The buckets are the size classes the population was actually analysed with,
so a --nclust of more than three no longer loses every call past the third class, as the
Perl's hardcoded A/B/C did.  A site where no run could have been called for anyone -- an
unanalysed chromosome, an assembly gap, an excluded region, outside the marker span -- is
UNASSESSED rather than NONE; folding those together inflates the outside-runs count by
however much of the genome the analysis left out.

Four differences from the scripts are worth stating, because each was a silent wrong answer
rather than a missing feature:

- An individual in the genotype file with no calls in the ROH file had every homozygote
  counted as outside a run, with no warning, because the Perl's interval lookup
  autovivified an empty list.  A sample-ID mismatch therefore produced a complete,
  plausible, entirely wrong table.  garlic now reports individuals each file has and the
  other does not, and stops if they have none in common.
- Calls in a fourth or later size class were counted into no column at all.
- The tracked copies began their chromosome loop at a hardcoded chromosome 22, so as
  distributed they counted chromosome 22 only.
- Gzipped input was honoured when reading the sample list but not when reading the
  genotypes, so a .tped.gz was parsed as binary.

Counting happens in the run that makes the calls, so there is no intermediate file and no
coordinate convention to agree on.  The genotypes are re-read from the input rather than
taken from garlic's filtered matrix, which by then has had monomorphic sites dropped per
population, has been pruned by --chr and --autosomes-only and has had excluded regions
removed -- it would have discarded exactly the rare functional sites this counts.  When the
classified variants live in a different file from the calls, --tped-counting with
--tfam-counting, or --vcf-counting, reads them; the two files need not share a site.  From a
VCF, indels and multiallelic sites are counted (the calling reader skips both), and the
classified allele is matched against REF and every ALT, so a class carried by the reference
allele is expressible -- the old format could not say that at all.

--roh-file counts against a .roh.bed that already exists, calling nothing.  A .roh.bed does
not record which chromosomes were analysed, how far the markers reached, or what the size
class letters mean, so that mode cannot mark a site UNASSESSED and reports its size class
boundaries as unknown; the header says so.  Counting inside the calling run knows all three.


Wrong answers, produced silently and with exit 0:

- The genetic map was paired with the data by position in the file rather than by
  chromosome name, so a map whose chromosomes appear in a different order than the TPED
  applied the wrong chromosome's map to every site. A lexically ordered copy of the bundled
  example gave 3525 ROH where the correct answer is 5738.
- filterMonomorphicSites and filterMonomorphicAndOOBSites overwrote each locus name with
  its physical position.
- getMapInfo interpolated on uninitialised stack values when query positions were not in
  ascending order.
- A failed LOD cutoff search returned -1 and that value was used as the cutoff, producing a
  complete, plausible-looking .roh.bed.
- assembleROHWindows wrote one element past the end of its window array.
- --auto-winsize aborted with a use-after-free in subsetData, after writing output, so it
  looked like it had worked. This was the first command in example/example_README.txt.
- get_arg_max seeded its running maximum with numeric_limits<double>::min(), which is the
  smallest positive value rather than the most negative.
- KDE modes were located by testing floating-point equality against the density array.

Reproducibility. Automatic LOD cutoff selection is now deterministic: --kde-subsample
defaults to 0 (every individual), which removed the last consumer of random numbers on that
path, and the KDE evaluates its Gaussian sum directly instead of calling figtree, whose
clustering made results depend on more than the seed. Three runs that drew three different
seeds now select the same cutoff and call the same ROH. --seed remains for the paths that
still sample. The figtree and ANN archives are no longer linked.

Performance, on the bundled example (577,489 loci, 45 individuals), same output:
10.1 s to 1.6 s, and peak memory 377 MB to 287 MB. The macOS build was passing -fast, which
clang does not accept as an optimisation flag, so it had been compiling at -O0; one Makefile
now detects the platform instead of three being hand-edited. Pairwise LD is evaluated once
rather than once per window, the four possible per-locus LOD values are precomputed instead
of calling log10 per genotype, TPED parsing no longer goes through std::istream, bulk arrays
are allocated as one contiguous block with genotypes stored as one byte instead of two, and
LOD calculation and ROH assembly are threaded.

VCF input. garlic reads a VCF directly with --vcf: plain, gzipped, or BGZF as written by
bgzip or bcftools. Biallelic SNVs with a GT field are used; multiallelic sites, indels,
symbolic ALTs and records with no GT in FORMAT are skipped and counted in the log. Records
for a chromosome must be contiguous, which is what bcftools sort produces. The VCF path
checks things a TPED cannot state: ploidy per call, phase per call (so --phased requires
'|' rather than silently mis-reading a '/'), and sample names from the #CHROM line -- so
--tfam is not accepted with --vcf and --pop supplies population labels instead. The ALT
allele is the one counted, so frequencies may come out as 1-f relative to the same data in
a TPED; this changes no call, and a .freq.gz file remains interchangeable between the two
readers because readFreqData re-orients on the ALLELE column. Verified by converting the
bundled chr21 TPED to a VCF with REF/ALT chosen alphabetically, which flips the dosage at
4,390 of 8,599 loci: both paths call the same 171 ROH with a bit-identical raw LOD matrix.

--pop maps sample ID to population, and works with --tped as well. Rows are matched by
sample ID, never by position; the column order is <id> <pop>, the opposite of a TFAM's, and
a TFAM-ordered file is diagnosed as such. An optional third column carries PLINK sex coding.
Population labels are not cosmetic -- they name the UCSC track and are column 2 of --froh.

*** Genotype likelihoods from a VCF were being used incorrectly, and silently. *** A VCF
normalises PL and GL so the CALLED genotype's value is exactly 0. The .tgls format holds one
value per genotype, and under --gl-type PL/GL that value is read as the probability the
genotype is correct -- so a VCF-derived file gave an error rate of 0 for every genotype,
clamped to 1e-16, which is a LOD of -16 per heterozygote; on chr21 that called 113 ROH
instead of 171. An all-integer PL file, which is what a VCF actually contains, gave error
rates of 0, 0.206, 0.369, 0.499, ... -- 0.001 would need PL = 0.00435, so no integer PL
expresses a realistic rate -- and called nothing at all. Both are now hard errors naming the
chromosome, position and sample, as are GQ < 0, PL < 0, GL > 0, and an unparseable value
(which previously left the value at 0 and failed the stream, so the rest of the line
silently became zeros).

--vcf --gl-type PL/GL is the correct path: it reads the whole FORMAT array and computes
P(g) proportional to 10^(-PL_g/10), error = 1 - P(called)/sum. A PL of (0,30,60) gives
0.001, exactly GQ 30. If the requested field is absent the run stops and names whichever of
GQ, PL and GL the record does carry. vcf2tped.pl is deprecated for this reason and prints a
notice; --gl-type GQ is the one form its output still works with, because GQ needs no
normalisation.

Physical positions are 64-bit throughout, so assemblies past 2.147 Gb no longer wrap
negative. The last two 32-bit truncations, in the --freq-only writer and in writeFreqData,
are fixed.

garlic no longer links GNU GSL, FIGTree or ANN. The ten GSL routines it used are reproduced
bit for bit in garlic-math.cpp, which removes 44 MB of vendored archives from every clone
and the largest source of build friction. Windows users no longer need the FIGTree DLLs.

A test suite is included: sh test/run_tests.sh runs 252 checks (253 where bgzip is installed),
one of which is a unit-test binary of a further 337 assertions -- golden-output regression,
mode smoke tests, exit-code and --load-params round-trip contracts, and the VCF
equivalences above -- from tracked data only. A GitHub Actions workflow builds on ubuntu
(shared and static) and macOS, runs the suite, runs every mode under ASan and UBSan, checks
that the generated command line reference in this file and manual/flags.tex is in sync with
the help strings, and runs every command in example/example_README.txt.

Command line. Exit codes are now 0 for success, 1 for a usage error, and 2 for a runtime
error; a misspelled flag used to exit 0, and argument errors exited 255. A rejected command
line no longer creates .log and .error files. New: --version, -h, --force (refuses to
overwrite existing calls without it), --seed, --quiet, --verbose, --chr, --outdir, --froh,
--load-params, --max-winsize, --kde-thin-step, --kde-points, --kde-cut, --mode-smooth-span,
--auto-winsize-threshold, --auto-winsize-coef, --auto-overlap-coef, --gmm-max-iter,
--gmm-tol. The last eight expose constants that were literals in the source and that each
move the selected cutoff or the size class boundaries; the two regression coefficient pairs
are empirical fits to human SNP-array data, which matters if you are not working on human
array data. --no-kde-thinning is now a deprecated alias for --kde-thin-step 1.

Every run writes <out>.params.json recording the effective value of every flag plus the
values the run settled on, including the drawn seed; --load-params replays it, with command
line flags taking precedence. --froh writes a per-individual autozygous total and fraction
by size class, with the denominator stated in the file header. The progress bar is drawn
only when stderr is a terminal, since it works by emitting backspaces. Size classes beyond
nine are no longer all drawn the same grey, and class labels no longer run past 'Z'.
--freq-only no longer requires a --winsize it never uses. Too few ROH for the size-class
GMM, and GSL domain errors inside it, are now reported instead of killing the process with
SIGABRT.

The command line reference in this file and in the manual is generated from the program's
own help strings by 'make docs' in src/. It had drifted to documenting 33 of 53 flags, and
described one flag the program has never accepted.

05JUN2020 - v1.1.6a Fixes a bug when using --auto-overlap-frac that can cause segmentation faults when not using chromosomes/contigs with numbers. ***I'm having trouble getting mscOS versions to compile, so the current binary for mscOS is still v1.6.0 and still has this bug.***

24AUG2017 - v1.1.6 Modified behavior of --cm flag based on user feedback. When this flag is set ROH lengths and length classifications will be reported in cM but ROH boundaries will be reported in bps.

18JUL2017 - v1.1.5 Introduced --ld-subsample argument. If set > 0, will use only a subset of individuals for computing LD statistics when calculating wLOD scores. 

18MAY2017 - v1.1.4 If a freq file is provided, GARLIC now ensures that internal coding of alleles is consistent with the allele specified in the file. Improved handling of loci with 100% missing data. Improved reading of gzipped freq files. Improved lod score cutoff discovery. Removed default setting for --winsize, which must now be specified. Now allows --auto-winsize to be used with --winsize-multi, will choose best from the list.

12MAY2017 - v1.1.3 adds a new option for --gl-type called GQ, which is a phred-scaled probability that the genotype is wrong. If p = 0.999 is the probability that the genotype is correct, then GQ = -10*log10(1-p) = 30.  The default for --gl-type is now set to none, so if you provide a TGLS file you will be required to choose between GQ, PL, and GL.

08MAY2017 - v1.1.2b fixes a bug that causes a crash when --overlap-frac is set to 0. Setting to zero now chooses the smallest sensible overlap fraction (1/winsize).  If you wish to let garlic automatically guess overlap fraction, use the new command line option: --auto-overlap-frac. Also includes an interation counter to monitor progress of GMM portion of the code.

07MAY2017 - v1.1.2 fixes a bug that causes a crash when a tgls file indicates a genotype has 0 likelihood. Add progress bars for LD calculations and LOD score calculations. Improve efficiency of tgls file loading.

06MAY2017 - v1.1.1a fixes a bug introduced with 1.1.1 that caused crashed when --resample is used.

05MAY2017 - Update to version 1.1.1. For some large datasets (e.g. WGS), KDE can take extremely long to complete, even when only a subset of individuals are considered.  Here we introduce some changes to speed this calculation up.  Instead of passing all LOD score windows to the KDE function, we now pass only non-overlapping windows.  This reduces the number of points sent to the function by a factor of WINSIZE. If you wish to retain all LOD scores, you may set the --no-kde-thinning flag. We also change the default number of random individuals for KDE (--kde-subsample) to 20, which still may be set to 0 to use all individuals. 

We also introduce a lower bound on the size of ROH reported.  Now, an ROH will not be called unless it contains at least WINSIZE*OVERLAP_FRAC number of SNPs. For example, for a WINSIZE (--winsize) of 100 and an OVERLAP_FRAC (--overlap-frac) of 0.25 the smallest possible ROH that will be reported will be 25 SNPs long.

Finally, if --overlap-frac is set to 0, GARLIC will attempt to guess a good choice.

02MAY2017 - Update to version 1.1.0a. Bug fixed that caused crashes when using TGLS file. 

28APR2017 - Update to version 1.1.0.  Includes several updates, including a method for reweighting LOD scores by gaps between SNPs, the option to report ROH lengths in cM, and the ability to provide per-genotype error rates.

25OCT2016 - Update to version 1.0.1.  This update fixes a bug where ROH that extended upto the end of the chromosome failed to get assembled and reported.
This update also introduces a new command-line flag --overlap-frac which is designed to reduce false positive calls by requiring a SNP be covered by at 
least OVERLAP_FRAC (default 0.25) proportion of high scoring windows to be included in an ROH call.  This helps to reduce false positive calls near the 
boundaries of true ROH.

garlic v2.1.0 -- a program to call runs of homozygosity in genetic data.
Source code and binaries can be found at <https://www.github.com/szpiech/garlic>.

Citations:

ZA Szpiech, et al. (2017) Bioinformatics, doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/btx102.
TJ Pemberton, et al. (2012) AJHG, 91: 275–292.

----------Command Line Arguments----------

#BEGIN GENERATED FLAG REFERENCE -- regenerate with 'make docs' in src/
This section is generated by 'make docs' from the program's own help
strings. Do not edit by hand.

----- Input files -----

--freq-file <string>: A file specifying allele frequencies for each population for all variants. File format: CHR SNP POS ALLELE FREQ <chr> <locus ID> <allele> <freq> By default, this is calculated automatically from the provided data.
	Default: none

--gl-beagle <string>: Genotype likelihoods in beagle format, as written by ANGSD -doGlf 2: a header of marker, allele1, allele2 and three columns per individual, then one row per site with P(reads | allele1/allele1), P(reads | allele1/allele2) and P(reads | allele2/allele2).  This is the input for low-coverage sequence data.  The window LOD is formed by marginalising over the triplet rather than from a called genotype, so an uncertain site contributes little evidence instead of confident wrong evidence, and a site with no reads (a flat triplet) contributes exactly 0.  Replaces --tped; --tfam is still required for individual IDs, population labels and sex, matched to the beagle columns by position (ANGSD's -b bam list order).  No --tped, --vcf or map file is read.  --error is still required and keeps its meaning: it is the autozygous state's allowance for a site that looks non-autozygous for any reason (mutation, gene conversion, mis-mapping, calling error) and is what a confidently heterozygous site inside a run costs; the likelihoods carry the read-level uncertainty, so the two are not double counted, and --gl-type (a quality turned into a per-site error) is refused. Allele frequencies are estimated by EM over the likelihoods, which is unbiased at low depth where frequencies from called genotypes are not; --freq-file (e.g. a column of ANGSD's .mafs.gz) overrides them. Three cautions.  Run ANGSD on an ascertained site list (-sites, or at least -SNP_pval); without one every covered position is in the file and invariant sites get error-driven frequencies.  Give LIKELIHOODS, not posteriors: ANGSD -doPost/-doGeno output has the Hardy-Weinberg prior already folded in, and feeding it here counts that prior twice.  And beagle markers carry physical positions only, so --weighted and --cm still need --map.  Below roughly 2x the automatic LOD cutoff cannot be located whatever the input; see the manual.
	Default: none

--gl-type <string>: Which genotype-quality field to use.  With --vcf it is read from the FORMAT column; with --tgls it names the form of the single value per genotype in the .tgls file.  The two sources are NOT equivalent.
With --vcf (recommended): GQ error = 10^(-GQ/10), the VCF spec's definition directly. PL The whole PL array is read and converted to a posterior:     P(g) proportional to 10^(-PL_g/10),  error = 1 - P(called)/sum.   This is the only correct use of a VCF's PL.  A VCF normalises PL   so the CALLED genotype is exactly 0, so that one value carries no   information -- it is 0 for every call, confident or not.  The   information is in how much worse the alternatives are. GL The same, via PL = -10*GL.   A missing field is an error naming whichever of GQ/PL/GL the file   does carry, and a called genotype with '.' for the field is an   error rather than a silent fall back to --error.
With --tgls (legacy; the format holds ONE value per genotype): GQ As above.  The only form a VCF's values can be dropped into. PL error = 1 - 10^(-PL/10), i.e. P(genotype CORRECT).   *** NOT the VCF PL field. *** Under this definition PL=0 means an   error of 0, and the integers map to 0, 0.21, 0.37, 0.50, ... --   none a realistic error rate (0.001 would be PL=0.00435).  Feeding   VCF PLs here is rejected.  Use --vcf --gl-type PL instead. GL error = 1 - 10^GL, with the same caveat.
Below roughly 4x, use --gl-beagle instead.  Every form above reduces to one error rate per genotype and is evaluated at the called genotype, so an uncertain homozygous call still counts as evidence for autozygosity, only with a smaller weight.  With GQ at 0.5-1x in the resampling simulation this called essentially the whole genome autozygous.  In the read-level benchmark --vcf --gl-type PL matched --gl-beagle down to 1x where the planted runs were several megabases long and the cohort carried many of them, and it found no usable cutoff at 1x in a population of low diversity (Ne 2,000).  How far down it holds depends on run length and on how much autozygosity the cohort carries, not on depth alone.  It also depends on the genotype caller, because the called genotype is the one evaluated: on the benchmark reads, removing the population prior from bcftools call left the 1x density with no mode below zero.  --gl-beagle averages over all three genotypes and is the intended input for such data.
	Default: none

--map <string>: Provide a scaffold genetic map, sites that aren't present within this file are interpolated. Sites outside the bounds are filtered. This is required for wLOD calculations and any runs for which you wish to report ROH in units of cM.
	Default: none

--tfam <string>: A tfam formatted file containing population and individual IDs.
	Default: none

--tgls <string>: A tgls file containing one per-genotype likelihood value per individual, in the same row order as the tped.  See --gl-type for what the values must mean: for PL and GL this is NOT the VCF convention.
	Default: none

--tped <string>: A tped formatted file containing map and genotype information.
	Default: none

--tped-missing <char>: Single character missing data code for TPED files.
	Default: 0

--vcf <string>: A VCF file (plain or gzipped) to read genotypes from, instead of --tped. Biallelic SNVs with a GT field are used; multiallelic sites, indels and symbolic ALTs are skipped and the counts reported. Sample names come from the #CHROM line, so --tfam is neither needed nor accepted; use --pop to supply population labels.
Sites must be grouped by chromosome. Ploidy must be 2, except on a chromosome declared with --sex-system or --sex-chr, where a haploid call is read as hemizygous. With --phased every call must use '|'.
	Default: none

--vcf-pass-only <bool>: Skip sites whose FILTER column is neither PASS nor '.'. Without this, such sites are KEPT and their number is reported, because a FILTER column reflects the caller's thresholds rather than garlic's.
	Default: false

----- Populations -----

--pool-populations <bool>: Analyse every individual together as one population, ignoring the population labels.  This is what garlic did before per-population analysis, and it restores the old output names as well: <out>.roh.bed rather than <out>.<POP>.roh.bed.
Allele frequencies are then computed by pooling everyone, which inflates heterozygosity relative to any one population and biases the LOD scores for all of them.  Use it when the labels are not populations -- column 1 of a TFAM is the PLINK family ID, which is often one family or even one sample per value -- or to reproduce an earlier run.
	Default: false

--pop <string>: A file mapping sample ID to population, replacing the population labels that would otherwise come from the TFAM. Whitespace separated, '#' comments and blank lines ignored, .gz accepted:
  <sample_id>  <population>  [sex]
Two required columns in THAT order. Note this is the opposite order to a TFAM, whose first column is the population. The optional third column is sex in PLINK coding (1 male, 2 female, 0 or -9 unknown); unlike a TFAM, anything else is an error rather than being read as unknown.
Rows are matched by sample ID, never by file order. Every sample in the data must have a row; extra rows are ignored and counted, so a cohort-wide file can be used against a subset.
	Default: none

----- Genome and chromosomes -----

--autosomes-only <bool>: Drop every chromosome that is not an autosome before calling: X, Y, Z, W and the mitochondrion, recognised by name, plus 23, 24, 25 and 26 when --build names a human assembly. A hemizygous genotype is written as a homozygous call and is indistinguishable from true autozygosity, so a hemizygous chromosome is otherwise called as one run spanning its whole length. Does not resolve bare 23-26 without a human --build: dropping them is right if they are PLINK's human codes and destroys real autosomes if they are not, so garlic refuses instead. See --sex-system.
	Default: false

--build <string>: Choose which genome build to use for centromere locations: hg18, hg19, hg38 or t2t-chm13 (T2T-CHM13v2.0, UCSC hs1).
	Default: none

--centromere <string>: Provide custom centromere boundaries. Format <chr> <start> <end>.
	Default: none

--chr <string1> ... <stringN>: Analyse only these chromosomes, e.g. --chr chr1 chr2 chrX. Names are matched after normalisation, so '1' and 'chr1' are the same. It is an error to name a chromosome that is not in the data.
	Default: all chromosomes in the input

--chr-lengths <string>: A file of chromosome lengths: two whitespace-separated columns, chromosome and length. A samtools faidx .fai is accepted unchanged -- its first two columns are already name and length and the rest is ignored -- so the index beside your reference can be passed as it is. The way to use --froh-denominator assembly on a non-human assembly, and on TPED input, where nothing in the data states a chromosome's length. Takes precedence over --build and over a VCF's ##contig header.
	Default: none

--exclude-regions <string>: A BED file of regions whose sites are dropped before anything is computed: a mappability or repeat mask, segmental duplications, or any region where calls are not to be trusted.  Mapping bias is regional -- repeats and structurally divergent segments mis-map reads toward the reference and turn heterozygotes into homozygotes there, which is what autozygosity looks like -- and the read-level part that remains after masking did not move the calls in simulation, so this is the one correction garlic applies for it.  Standard BED: 0-based half-open <chrom> <chromStart> <chromEnd>, further columns ignored, track/browser/# lines skipped, may be gzipped. Chromosomes it names that are not in the data are ignored.  The dropped sites are gone from the frequency file and the window count; the FROH denominator is not reduced by them (unlike --par), since a run of homozygosity can still span a short masked region.  Applied on every chromosome, autosomes included; use --par for pseudoautosomal regions.
	Default: none

--haploid-chr <string1> ... <stringN>: Chromosomes haploid in every individual: mitochondrion, chloroplast. Dropped before calling, since a run of homozygosity on them is not a meaningful quantity.
	Default: names matching M or MT, plus 26 under a human --build

--no-centromere <bool>: Treat every chromosome as having no assembled gap, instead of requiring --build or --centromere. For organisms with no centromere gap in the assembly, and for references garlic has no built-in table for (only hg18, hg19 and hg38 are built in; T2T-CHM13 is not). Mutually exclusive with --build and --centromere.
	Default: false

--par <string1> ... <stringN>: Pseudoautosomal regions on the shared sex chromosome, as <chr>:<start>-<end>, comma or space separated. They are diploid in both sexes and too short to call at a useful window size, so the loci inside them are dropped. ANY NUMBER of regions per chromosome: humans have two, other species have more, and a system with several shared sex chromosomes can have them on each. Coordinates are read in the assembly the input uses; there is deliberately no way to name a pseudoautosomal region by chromosome code, because PLINK's 25 is an ordinary autosome in most species. --build supplies the human X regions on its own, from the Genome Reference Consortium's definition for that assembly; see centromeres/par_regions.txt. Naming any region here replaces them, and --par none keeps them all.
	Default: the regions for --build, or none without one

--par-file <string>: The same regions from a file, one per line, <chr> <start> <end>. '#' comments and blank lines are ignored and .gz is accepted. Unlike --centromere, repeated rows for a chromosome ADD a region rather than replacing one. May be given together with --par; the regions accumulate.
	Default: none

--sex-chr <string1> ... <stringN>: The chromosome diploid in the homogametic sex and hemizygous in the heterogametic one: X under xy, Z under zw. Only needed when the name is not the conventional one, e.g. --sex-system zw --sex-chr LG12. Requires --sex-system.
	Default: the conventional name for --sex-system

--sex-chr-degenerate <string1> ... <stringN>: The chromosome carried only by the heterogametic sex: Y under xy, W under zw. No individual can carry a run of homozygosity on it -- hemizygous in one sex and absent in the other -- so it is dropped before calling.
	Default: the conventional name for --sex-system

--sex-system <string>: Which sex is heterogametic, and therefore which individuals are hemizygous on the shared sex chromosome:   xy    individuals coded 1 (male) in the TFAM or --pop are heterogametic   zw    individuals coded 2 (female) are heterogametic   none  assert that every chromosome is diploid in every individual, and         stop reporting sex chromosomes entirely PLINK sex coding is 1 male and 2 female whatever the species' sex determination system is, so in a ZW species the HOMOGAMETIC sex is coded 1. This is the one thing no amount of genotype data can supply, which is why it is a flag. It is not an analysis path: it fills in a per-chromosome, per-individual expected ploidy that one code path then reads. Leaving it unset is NOT the same as 'none'. If a sex chromosome is present and this is unset, garlic stops rather than analysing it as an autosome.
	Default: unset

----- LOD scores and windows -----

--M <int>: The expected number of meioses since a recent common ancestor for --weighted calculation.
	Default: 7

--auto-winsize <bool>: If --weighted is set, guesses the best window size based on SNP density, otherwise initiates an ad hoc method for automatically selecting the # of SNPs in which to calculate LOD scores. Starts at the value specified by --winsize and increases by <step size> SNPs until finished.
	Default: false

--auto-winsize-coef <double1> ... <doubleN>: <slope> <intercept>: coefficients of winsize = slope*log(density) + intercept, used by --auto-winsize with --weighted. The defaults (8.3235 138.0521) are an empirical fit to human SNP-array data; override them for other ascertainment schemes or species.
	Default: 8.3235 138.0521

--auto-winsize-step <int>: Step size for automatic window selection algorithm.
	Default: 10

--auto-winsize-threshold <double>: Smoothness criterion at which the --auto-winsize search stops. Lower values demand a smoother LOD score density and therefore a larger window.
	Default: 0.5

--error <double>: The assumed genotyping error rate.
	Default: -1

--max-gap <int>: A LOD score window is not calculated if the gap (in bps) between two loci is greater than this value.
	Default: 200000

--max-winsize <int>: Upper bound on the window size that --auto-winsize will try. The search previously had no bound and would grow past the number of loci if the smoothness criterion was never met.
	Default: 1000

--mu <double>: Mutation rate per bp per generation for --weighted calculation.
	Default: 1e-09

--phased <bool>: Set if data are phased and you want to calculate r2 instead of hr2 while --weighted is set. Uses extra RAM. Has no effect on computations without --weighted.
	Default: false

--weighted <bool>: Compute LOD scores weighted by LD and probability of mutation.
	Default: false

--winsize <int>: The window size in # of SNPs in which to calculate LOD scores.
	Default: 0

--winsize-multi <int1> ... <intN>: Provide several window sizes (in # of SNPs) to calculate LOD scores. LOD score KDEs for each window size will be output for inspection.
	Default: -1

----- Calling runs of homozygosity -----

--auto-overlap-coef <double1> ... <doubleN>: <slope> <intercept>: coefficients of overlap% = slope*log(density) + intercept, used by --auto-overlap-frac. The defaults (6.375 63.888) are an empirical fit to human SNP-array data.
	Default: 6.375 63.888

--auto-overlap-frac <bool>: If set, GARLIC attempts to guess based on marker density.
	Default: false

--het-rate-bounds <double1> ... <doubleN>: <lo> <hi>: heterozygosity rates on the shared sex chromosome below which an individual reads as hemizygous and above which it reads as diploid. Between them it is ambiguous, and garlic neither calls ROH there for that individual nor counts its alleles. Used to check every recorded sex and to infer the ones that were not recorded. Fixed bounds rather than a two-component fit on purpose: a cohort that is entirely one sex has no bimodality to find, and a clustering rule would invent a split there. These fail the same way for every dataset.
	Default: 0.020000

--ld-subsample <int>: The number of individuals to randomly sample for LD calculation during wLOD. If there are fewer individuals in the population all are used. Set <= 0 to use all individuals (will increase runtime).
	Default: 0

--lod-cutoff <double>: For LOD based ROH calling, specify a single LOD score cutoff above which ROH are called in all populations.  By default, this is chosen automatically with KDE.
	Default: -999999

--overlap-frac <double>: The minimum fraction of overlapping windows above the LOD cutoff required to begin constructing a run. ROH will have a lower bound size threshold of WINSIZE*OVERLAP_FRAC. If set to 0, GARLIC sets the value to the lowest sensible value: 1/winsize.
	Default: 0.25

--sexchr-lod-cutoff <double>: Call the shared sex chromosome at this LOD score cutoff instead of the one estimated on the autosomes. Every run logs what the sex chromosome's own windows would have given, which is where the value for this comes from. The autosomal cutoff is the default because one chromosome's windows from part of a cohort often cannot locate a second minimum in the density at all; that is also why this is a value you supply rather than a switch that asks garlic to re-estimate.
	Default: the autosomal cutoff

--sexchr-winsize <int>: Build windows of this many SNPs on the shared sex chromosome instead of the size used on the autosomes. The overlap FRACTION is unchanged, so the number of windows a SNP must fall in scales with the window. A LOD score cutoff belongs to the window size it was estimated at, so with this the autosomal cutoff no longer applies to the sex chromosome; give --sexchr-lod-cutoff as well.
	Default: the autosomal window size

----- Choosing the LOD cutoff automatically -----

--kde-cut <double>: Extend the density grid this many bandwidths beyond the range of the data.
	Default: 3

--kde-points <int>: Number of grid points at which the LOD score density is evaluated. Also sets the resolution at which the between-mode minimum (the LOD cutoff) is located.
	Default: 512

--kde-subsample <int>: The number of individuals to randomly sample for LOD score KDE. If there are fewer individuals in the population all are used. The default, 0, uses every individual, which makes the selected LOD cutoff a deterministic function of the data alone. A positive value subsamples, which saves memory but makes the cutoff depend on --seed.
	Default: 0

--kde-thin-step <int>: Take every Nth LOD score window when building the KDE. 0 (the default) uses the window size, which keeps the sampled windows non-overlapping; 1 uses every window. Replaces --no-kde-thinning, which is equivalent to 1 and is kept as a deprecated alias.
	Default: 0

--mode-smooth-span <int>: Number of adjacent grid points used to smooth the density when locating its modes. Must be smaller than --kde-points.
	Default: 20

--no-kde-thinning <bool>: Set this flag to send all LOD score data to KDE function. This may dramatically increase runtime.
	Default: false

--stratify-cutoff <bool>: Choose the automatic LOD cutoff within strata of individuals that carry the same evidence per window.  The cutoff is normally the valley between the two modes of the window LOD density pooled over everyone, which is right when every individual carries the same evidence per window and wrong when they do not: an individual at low depth, or with many missing genotypes, has both modes compressed toward zero and a cutoff placed for the cohort calls most of its genome autozygous.  The location of an individual's non-autozygous mode is a precise measure of that evidence, so with this flag individuals are sorted by it and neighbours whose modes differ by less than a quarter of the smaller spread share a stratum; the pooled rule is then applied within each stratum.  A cohort of uniform depth is one stratum and gets exactly the pooled cutoff; a mixed cohort splits by depth.  A stratum whose density has no valley borrows the others' median standardised valley through its own mode and spread.  Per-individual mode, spread, stratum and applied cutoff are written to <out>.ind.tsv; the .kde file is the largest stratum's.  Needs the automatic cutoff.
	Default: false

----- Size classification -----

--cm <bool>: Measure ROH lengths in genetic distance units. This requires a mapfile.
	Default: false

--gmm-max-iter <int>: Maximum EM iterations when fitting ROH size classes.
	Default: 1000

--gmm-tol <double>: Convergence tolerance for the ROH size-class EM fit.
	Default: 1e-05

--nclust <int>: Set number of clusters for GMM classification of ROH lengths.
	Default: 3

--size-bounds <double1> ... <doubleN>: Specify the size class boundaries ROH boundaries.  By default, this is chosen automatically with a 3-component GMM.  Must provide numbers in increasing order.
	Default: -1.000000

----- Counting classified genotypes -----

--features <string>: A file classifying sites, four whitespace-separated columns: <chr> <pos> <allele> <class>. Lines beginning '#' are comments.
The class label is arbitrary text and there may be any number of them (deleterious/tolerated, synonymous/nonsynonymous, whatever the annotation gives). A site may appear more than once, so two annotation schemes can classify the same variant; the same class twice at one site is an error. Chromosome names are matched ignoring case and a 'chr' prefix.
For each individual, garlic then counts the classified genotypes falling inside runs of homozygosity of each size class and outside them, and writes <out>.counts.tsv. Genotypes are read from the input the run was called from, or from --tped-counting if that is given.
	Default: none

--roh-file <string>: Count classified genotypes against calls in an existing garlic .roh.bed instead of calling them. No ROH are called, so --tped and --vcf are not used; give the genotypes with --tped-counting and --tfam-counting, or with --vcf-counting, and the classification with --features.
A .roh.bed records where the runs are, not which chromosomes were analysed or what the size class letters mean, so the table cannot mark a site UNASSESSED and its size class boundaries are reported as unknown. Counting inside the run that makes the calls knows both.
garlic 1.1.6a was released with two different chromStart conventions under the same version string, so the convention is recovered from the file itself and reported.
	Default: none

--tfam-counting <string>: The TFAM naming the samples in --tped-counting, in column order. Individuals are matched to the ROH calls by ID; one present in only one of the two files is reported and left out of the table.
	Default: none

--tped-counting <string>: A TPED to count classified genotypes from, instead of the file the run was called from. Use it when the calls come from array data and the classified variants are sequenced -- the two need not share a single site. Requires --tfam-counting and --features.
These genotypes take no part in calling, and no site filter is applied to them: monomorphic and rare sites are exactly what this counts.
	Default: none

--vcf-counting <string>: A VCF to count classified genotypes from, instead of the file the run was called from. Sample names come from its #CHROM line. Requires --features.
Unlike the VCF the run is CALLED from, indels and multiallelic sites are read here: the classified allele is matched against REF and every ALT, so a classification that is not a biallelic SNV still counts.
	Default: none

----- Output -----

--force <bool>: Overwrite existing output files. Without this, garlic refuses to clobber an existing <out>.roh.bed.
	Default: false

--freq-only <bool>: If set, calculates a freq file from provided data and then exits. Uses minimal RAM.
	Default: false

--froh <bool>: Also write <out>.froh.tsv: per-individual autozygous total and fraction, broken down by ROH size class. The denominator is stated in the file header.
	Default: false

--froh-denominator <string>: What <out>.froh.tsv divides the autozygous total by. analyzed (default): the span the markers cover -- from the first to the last locus of each analysed chromosome, less the centromere and any region excluded with --par. This is what a marker set can support on its own, and it moves with the markers, so FROH from two different arrays are not directly comparable. assembly: the FULL length of each analysed chromosome, nothing subtracted. This is what published FROH denominators are, and it is reproducible from an external table rather than from the data. Lengths come from --chr-lengths, from --build, or from a VCF's ##contig header, in that order; a chromosome with no length available stops the run rather than falling back. Not available with --cm: a base pair length is not a genetic length.
	Default: analyzed

--out <string>: The base name for all output files.
	Default: outfile

--outdir <string>: Write all output files into this directory, which is created if it does not exist. Equivalent to prefixing --out with the path.

--raw-lod <bool>: If set, LOD scores will be output to gzip compressed files.
	Default: false

----- Run control -----

--dump-docs <string>: Write the command line reference to stdout in a documentation format and exit: txt for the README block, tex for a LaTeX description list. Used by 'make docs' so the documentation cannot drift from the program.
	Default: __none

--help <bool>: Prints this help dialog.
	Default: false

--load-params <string>: Read flag values from a <out>.params.json written by a previous run. Flags given on the command line take precedence over the file, so a run can be repeated with one parameter changed.
	Default: __none

--quiet <bool>: Suppress the progress bar and informational messages. Errors and warnings still go to stderr and to <out>.error.
	Default: false

--resample <int>: Number of resamples for estimating allele frequencies. When set to 0 (default), garlic will use allele frequencies as calculated from the data.
	Default: 0

--seed <int>: Random seed, for reproducible runs. Affects --kde-subsample, --ld-subsample and --resample. If 0 (default), a nondeterministic seed is drawn and written to the log file so the run can be repeated exactly.
	Default: 0

--threads <int>: The number of threads to use. Applies to LOD score calculation, the LD calculations under --weighted, the LOD score KDE, and ROH assembly.
	Default: 1

--verbose <bool>: Show the progress bar even when stderr is not a terminal. By default it is drawn only on a terminal, because it works by emitting backspaces.
	Default: false

--version <bool>: Print the version and exit.
	Default: false

-h <bool>: Prints this help dialog.
	Default: false

#END GENERATED FLAG REFERENCE
